;; THE REGISTRY OF COPIES. One file, read by every checker.
;;
;; This repository is the authority for four files, and other repositories
;; carry byte copies of them. Until 2026-09-03 the list of those copies existed
;; in FOUR places -- `src/kotoba/lang/grammar_authority.clj`,
;; `scripts/check-grammar-authority.cljs`, `lang/elaboration-pipeline.edn`'s
;; `:vendored-consumers`, and the deferral map in
;; `test/kotoba/lang/grammar_authority_test.clj` -- and they disagreed. The EDN
;; one named seven paths and knew nothing about kotoba-sema, kotoba's second
;; copy, or amu's elaboration-pipeline copy; the .cljs one named three. A
;; registry of copies that is itself copied is the defect it was written to
;; find.
;;
;; WHY EACH COPY EXISTS (measured 2026-09-03, per repository, by reading what
;; opens the file and whether the repository declares this one as a dependency):
;;
;;   kotoba-sema   NOT a dependant. `kotoba.compiler.frontend/load-catalog-
;;                 forbidden` and `load-capability-catalog` open the classpath
;;                 resource in PRODUCTION, and sema owns the frontend the
;;                 grammar describes. Its dependencies are artifact, kotoba-hir
;;                 and kotoba-kir; adding this repository would pull inga, ucan,
;;                 did, vc-data-integrity and identity into a frontend. This is
;;                 the copy that has the strongest reason to exist, and it is
;;                 the one that travels in the same commit as its reader.
;;
;;   grammar       NOT a dependant. The file is a BUILD INPUT: `tools/gen-
;;                 embedded.cljs` projects it into `src/kotoba/grammar/
;;                 embedded.cljc`, which is what `kotoba.grammar` reads at
;;                 runtime, and `--check` refuses drift between the two. A
;;                 generated-and-gitignored copy is not available to that repo's
;;                 own consumers, who resolve it as a git dependency and get
;;                 only what is committed.
;;
;;   kotoba        A DEPENDANT (`io.github.kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang` is pinned),
;;                 and it still ships two copies of its own. The first shadows
;;                 the dependency's; the second comes from `vendor/grammar`, a
;;                 committed FORK of the grammar repository whose
;;                 `kotoba/grammar.clj` still reads `io/resource` -- upstream
;;                 has been `.cljc` reading a compiled-in projection since
;;                 2026-08-18. Both are history nobody revisited, and undoing
;;                 them is a behaviour change in the one reader of
;;                 `:admitted-builtins`, so it is recorded as an owner decision
;;                 rather than taken here.
;;
;;   amu           NOT a dependant. Its guest-grammar copy was RETIRED
;;                 2026-09-03 (see `:retired`): nothing under `src/` read it,
;;                 the JDK-free nbb route has no classpath reader at all, and
;;                 its only effect was to SHADOW kotoba-sema's copy -- putting
;;                 the frontend's refusal set under a file in a different
;;                 repository from the frontend. Its capability-catalog and
;;                 elaboration-pipeline copies remain, for the same reason
;;                 kotoba-sema's do (no dependency edge) and with the same
;;                 shadowing hazard still open.
;;
;;   this repo     `lang/` is the authority and `resources/kotoba/lang/` is the
;;                 same bytes at the RESOURCE PATH consumers resolve. Two files
;;                 in one commit, so drift is a within-commit mistake rather
;;                 than a cross-repository wave; `local-and-sibling-vendors-
;;                 match-authority` refuses it and never defers it.
;;
;; `:checkout-path` is relative to this repository's root in the west monorepo
;; layout; a path that is absent there is `:missing` and tolerated, because a
;; sibling need not be checked out. `:repo` and `:path` are what
;; `scripts/check-vendored-copies-fleet.cljs` asks GitHub, which is the only
;; question whose answer does NOT depend on what happens to be checked out.

{:kotoba.lang.vendored-copies/version 1
 :kotoba.lang.vendored-copies/authority "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang"

 :copies
 [;; ---- lang/guest-grammar.edn ------------------------------------------
  {:authority "lang/guest-grammar.edn"
   :repo "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang"
   :path "resources/kotoba/lang/guest-grammar.edn"
   :checkout-path "resources/kotoba/lang/guest-grammar.edn"
   :exists-because :resource-path-in-the-authority-repo
   :read-by "kotoba.lang.guest-grammar/catalog"
   :dependant? true}
  {:authority "lang/guest-grammar.edn"
   :repo "kotoba-lang/kotoba-sema"
   :path "resources/kotoba/lang/guest-grammar.edn"
   :checkout-path "../kotoba-sema/resources/kotoba/lang/guest-grammar.edn"
   :exists-because :no-dependency-edge-and-read-in-production
   :read-by "kotoba.compiler.frontend/load-catalog-forbidden"
   :dependant? false}
  {:authority "lang/guest-grammar.edn"
   :repo "kotoba-lang/grammar"
   :path "resources/kotoba/lang/guest-grammar.edn"
   :checkout-path "../grammar/resources/kotoba/lang/guest-grammar.edn"
   :exists-because :build-input-for-a-committed-projection
   :read-by "tools/gen-embedded.cljs -> src/kotoba/grammar/embedded.cljc"
   :dependant? false}
  {:authority "lang/guest-grammar.edn"
   :repo "kotoba-lang/kotoba"
   :path "resources/kotoba/lang/guest-grammar.edn"
   :checkout-path "../kotoba/resources/kotoba/lang/guest-grammar.edn"
   :exists-because :history-nobody-revisited
   :read-by "kotoba.grammar/catalog, from the vendor/grammar fork"
   :dependant? true}
  {:authority "lang/guest-grammar.edn"
   :repo "kotoba-lang/kotoba"
   :path "vendor/grammar/resources/kotoba/lang/guest-grammar.edn"
   :checkout-path "../kotoba/vendor/grammar/resources/kotoba/lang/guest-grammar.edn"
   :exists-because :committed-fork-of-the-grammar-repository
   :read-by "the same reader; whichever of the two wins on the classpath"
   :dependant? true}

  ;; ---- lang/capability-catalog.edn -------------------------------------
  {:authority "lang/capability-catalog.edn"
   :repo "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang"
   :path "resources/kotoba/lang/capability-catalog.edn"
   :checkout-path "resources/kotoba/lang/capability-catalog.edn"
   :exists-because :resource-path-in-the-authority-repo
   :read-by "kotoba.lang.capability-catalog"
   :dependant? true}
  {:authority "lang/capability-catalog.edn"
   :repo "kotoba-lang/amu"
   :path "resources/kotoba/lang/capability-catalog.edn"
   :checkout-path "../amu/resources/kotoba/lang/capability-catalog.edn"
   :exists-because :no-dependency-edge
   :read-by "shadows kotoba-sema's copy for load-capability-catalog"
   :dependant? false}
  {:authority "lang/capability-catalog.edn"
   :repo "kotoba-lang/kotoba-sema"
   :path "resources/kotoba/lang/capability-catalog.edn"
   :checkout-path "../kotoba-sema/resources/kotoba/lang/capability-catalog.edn"
   :exists-because :no-dependency-edge-and-read-in-production
   :read-by "kotoba.compiler.frontend/load-capability-catalog"
   :dependant? false}

  ;; ---- lang/host-parity.edn --------------------------------------------
  {:authority "lang/host-parity.edn"
   :repo "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang"
   :path "resources/kotoba/lang/host-parity.edn"
   :checkout-path "resources/kotoba/lang/host-parity.edn"
   :exists-because :resource-path-in-the-authority-repo
   :read-by "host parity reporting"
   :dependant? true}
  {:authority "lang/host-parity.edn"
   :repo "kotoba-lang/kotoba"
   :path "resources/kotoba/lang/host-parity.edn"
   :checkout-path "../kotoba/resources/kotoba/lang/host-parity.edn"
   :exists-because :history-nobody-revisited
   :read-by "host parity reporting"
   :dependant? true}

  ;; ---- lang/elaboration-pipeline.edn -----------------------------------
  {:authority "lang/elaboration-pipeline.edn"
   :repo "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang"
   :path "resources/kotoba/lang/elaboration-pipeline.edn"
   :checkout-path "resources/kotoba/lang/elaboration-pipeline.edn"
   :exists-because :resource-path-in-the-authority-repo
   :read-by "kotoba.lang.grammar-authority"
   :dependant? true}
  {:authority "lang/elaboration-pipeline.edn"
   :repo "kotoba-lang/amu"
   :path "resources/kotoba/lang/elaboration-pipeline.edn"
   :checkout-path "../amu/resources/kotoba/lang/elaboration-pipeline.edn"
   :exists-because :no-dependency-edge
   :read-by "definition-identity-test, for :contract-versions :desugar-contract"
   :dependant? false}]

 ;; Checkout paths that must NOT resolve. A repository that was renamed leaves
 ;; a path that a stale checkout still answers on; a copy that was deleted for
 ;; a structural reason comes back the moment someone runs a resync script that
 ;; still knows the path. Both are named here so the return is a NAMED failure
 ;; rather than a silent restoration.
 :retired
 [{:authority "lang/guest-grammar.edn"
   :repo "kotoba-lang/amu"
   :path "resources/kotoba/lang/guest-grammar.edn"
   :checkout-path "../amu/resources/kotoba/lang/guest-grammar.edn"
   :retired "2026-09-03"
   :reason
   "amu shipped a copy that SHADOWED kotoba-sema's on its own classpath, and
    kotoba-sema owns the frontend that reads it. Nothing under amu's src/ ever
    opened the resource and its JDK-free route has no classpath reader, so the
    copy's only effect was to decide which grammar the frontend read -- from a
    different repository's commit. Deleted so the grammar and the frontend
    travel in one pin. Reintroducing it is a design change and belongs in an
    ADR, not in a resync."
   :refused-by "amu guest-grammar-vendor-test/this-repository-ships-no-copy-of-the-grammar"}
  {:authority "lang/guest-grammar.edn"
   :repo nil
   :path nil
   :checkout-path "../compiler/resources/kotoba/lang/guest-grammar.edn"
   :retired "2026-08"
   :reason "kotoba-lang/compiler was renamed to kotoba-lang/amu; a stale
            checkout may still answer under the old directory name."
   :refused-by nil}
  {:authority "lang/capability-catalog.edn"
   :repo nil
   :path nil
   :checkout-path "../compiler/resources/kotoba/lang/capability-catalog.edn"
   :retired "2026-08"
   :reason "same rename"
   :refused-by nil}
  {:authority "lang/elaboration-pipeline.edn"
   :repo nil
   :path nil
   :checkout-path "../compiler/resources/kotoba/lang/elaboration-pipeline.edn"
   :retired "2026-08"
   :reason "same rename"
   :refused-by nil}]

 ;; Copies KNOWN to be behind, each with a date, a reason and the condition
 ;; that closes it. A copy that is behind and is NOT here fails. An entry whose
 ;; copy is no longer behind ALSO fails -- see
 ;; `scripts/check-vendored-copies-fleet.cljs`, which can make that converse
 ;; assertion because it asks GitHub about the consumer's default branch, an
 ;; answer that does not depend on what happens to be checked out locally.
 ;; A bare skip list is not permitted: every key below carries all four fields.
 :deferrals
 {"kotoba-lang/kotoba-sema resources/kotoba/lang/capability-catalog.edn"
  {:as-of "2026-09-03"
   :reason
   "Found by the first run of the fleet check, having never been compared
    before: the registry listed the path and every local run tolerated its
    absence. And the divergence is not the expected direction -- the COPY is
    AHEAD. kotoba-sema c823461 added `:screen/observe` (wire id 31) and
    `:screen/act` (32) to its vendored copy of this authority; the authority
    itself does not name them. A consumer editing a vendored authority file is
    a language-surface change made in the wrong repository, and deciding it
    belongs to the owner rather than to a resync. Measured 2026-09-03:
    authority 72ea42e3, kotoba-sema 76964561. amu's copy of the same authority
    SHADOWS kotoba-sema's on amu's classpath, which is the same structural
    hazard that retired amu's guest-grammar copy -- so the two capability ids
    are invisible to the compiler that would have to lower them."
   :closes-when
   "the two capability entries land in kotoba-lang lang/capability-catalog.edn
    and kotoba-sema resyncs from it -- or the owner decides they should not,
    and kotoba-sema reverts its copy"}

  "kotoba-lang/kotoba resources/kotoba/lang/guest-grammar.edn"
  {:as-of "2026-09-03"
   :reason
   "MEASURED, not estimated. kotoba's copies all agree WITH EACH OTHER, and its
    own check is stricter than this one -- every classpath copy byte-identical,
    no exemption -- so resyncing the two it ships alone breaks it, rightly,
    because io/resource answers with whichever comes first. Closing the gap
    therefore means advancing the kotoba-lang and kotoba-sema pins in the same
    commit, and that was carried out and REVERTED on 2026-09-03 for a reason
    that is not about the grammar at all:

      The bytes 871f3873 and two new runnable conformance cases arrived in ONE
      authority commit (aacaffc), so the grammar cannot be taken without them.
      Both are `:required-backends #{:kir :wasm32-kotoba-v1}`, and kotoba's
      `authority-positive-cases-run-on-primary-wasm` refuses to record a
      wasm-required case as pending -- correctly. Its primary emitter
      (`kotoba.runtime/wasm-binary`) answers `:unsupported-op
      \"typed-set-contains\"` for :bounded-set-literal-and-operations and
      `:unsupported-op \"option-value-of\"` for :map-literal-value-types.
      Measured on the advanced pins: 703 tests, 9597 assertions, 4 failures --
      two of them these, two pre-existing `.isFile` checks under an absent
      ../kototama sibling.

    The refusals are emitter gaps, independent of the grammar bytes. Weakening
    that guard to let the resync through is the move kotoba's own ADR already
    recorded as a mistake once (an allowlist landing beside a stronger check
    that already existed), so it is not taken here."
   :closes-when
   "either kotoba's primary wasm emitter gains `typed-set-contains` and
    `option-value-of`, or an owner decides that
    `authority-positive-cases-run-on-primary-wasm` should not read
    `:required-backends #{... :wasm32-kotoba-v1}` as a demand on
    `kotoba.runtime/wasm-binary` -- kotoba's own ADR tabulates the two as
    different programs, and the authority's manifest records
    `:unexecuted-backends {:wasm32-kotoba-v1 ...}` for both cases with the note
    that amu owns that backend. Either way the two copies and the kotoba-lang
    and kotoba-sema pins then move in one commit."}

  "kotoba-lang/kotoba vendor/grammar/resources/kotoba/lang/guest-grammar.edn"
  {:as-of "2026-09-03"
   :reason "the second of kotoba's two shipped copies; see the entry above"
   :closes-when "the same commit"}}}
